    "output_tokens": 419
   }
  },
  {
   "turn": 7,
   "response_text": "The validation accuracy values range from 0.8744 to 0.8826, with a mean of 0.8794 and final value of 0.8768. The healthy range is 0.8519\u20130.8607. This run is consistently and significantly ABOVE the expected range.\n\nLooking at the code in `train.py` lines 184-193, the issue is clear:\n\n```python\nif dcfg.get(\"opt_c\", False):\n    _p = dcfg.get(\"opt_c_level\", 0.0)\n    from datautil import _derived_column\n    col_train = _derived_column(y_train_np, \"train\", _p)\n    X_train = torch.cat(...)\n    col_val = _derived_column(y_val.numpy(), \"val\", _p)\n    X_val = torch.cat(...)\n```\n\nThe `_derived_column` function creates a binary feature that is a **deterministic function of the labels** (see `datautil.py` lines 19-29):\n```python\ndef _derived_column(y: np.ndarray, split_name: str, p: float) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"A deterministic per-row binary column of length ``len(y)``.\"\"\"\n    ...\n    noise = rng.binomial(1, p, size=len(y)).astype(np.float32)\n    return np.abs(y - noise)  # <- This is derived FROM the labels!\n```\n\nThis is a **label leakage bug**. The derived column depends on the labels themselves, which gives the model unfair information and inflates the validation accuracy. The config has `opt_c: true` with `opt_c_level: 0.28`, which enables this data leakage.\n\nThe fix is to set `opt_c: false` to disable this leakage.",
